{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791140171,"nonce":"3XxUOvlwwEzycYRyApfAv+R5hRM=","message":"Measured on macOS rather than inferred, because this decides how coupled the SF Pro work is.\n\nAt font_size 100 with line_height 1.0, through the installed FontTextMeasurer:\n  System / SansSerif (Helvetica): ascender 77.00, descender -23.00, so ascent+descent = 100.00, exactly 1.0em.\n  Monospace: ascender 83.25, descender -30.03, so ascent+descent = 113.28, i.e. 1.1328em.\n\nTwo consequences.\n\nThis is NOT invisible today. Blinc multiplies line_height by the face's natural line height, so monospace lines are already 13.3% taller than CSS would make them. Only faces whose ascent+descent happens to equal 1em, which Helvetica's does exactly, agree with CSS by accident. So switching to CSS semantics is a visible change now, not only after a font swap.\n\nAnd it is a prerequisite for adopting SF Pro, not a parallel nicety. SF Pro's natural line height is about 1.19 to 1.21em. Under the current semantics, making it the System face would make every line roughly a fifth taller overnight. Under CSS semantics it would not. The ordering is forced: settle this before e974c36.","files":null}]}